spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/PLAN.md-35-
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/PLAN.md:36:## T28 — Membership integrity: memberships only through 
authorized server paths
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/PLAN.md:37:Reasoning: Every members-only guarantee of this phase (typing 
topic, mentions, roster, read state, messages, attachments, receipts) trusts `conversation_members`; owner decision D9 
puts the self-enrolment gap in scope (PLAN review r4 #1–#3 widened it to every client-reachable and server-side path 
that creates or restores a membership).
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/PLAN.md-38-Task: A `conversation_members` row is created or restored only 
by a server path that authorizes that user for that conversation (direct conversation creation among the users it 
names, under the existing company rules; room conversation membership per the room's access rules; an explicitly 
authorized room join/rejoin; test seeding). No anon or authenticated client — acting through `conversation_members`, 
`conversations` INSERT/UPDATE (`participants`, `room_id`, `type`), the participant sync trigger 
`sync_conversation_members`, the conversation resolver/join routes, or any other RPC, view, or trigger — can make 
itself or any other user a member it is not authorized to be, or move a membership to another conversation or user. A 
removed membership stays removed when unrelated writes happen (another user's join, a participants sync, 
preference/archive/read writes); only an authorized rejoin restores it. A grant (creation, join, rejoin — current 
service-role paths and legacy user-scoped writes alike) that races a committed revocation of that user's access (room 
permission, membership removal) creates no membership: authorization must still hold when the membership is written. 
Preference, archive, and read writes update an existing membership and never create one; a membership that vanished 
between authorization and the write is answered as not a participant with no row created. A client DELETE of its own 
membership has one enforced meaning — either it is refused, or it is a leave that is distinguished from administrative 
revocation and whose rejoin follows the normal authorization — and no client can delete another user's membership; the 
choice is recorded in TRACK. The hosted build and the Phase 4 build keep creating direct and room conversations, 
joining rooms, and changing preferences/archive/read state (including their user-scoped writes, e.g. preference 
upserts of an existing membership). The inventory of membership writers and the decision for each is recorded in TRACK.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/PLAN.md-39-Done when: On the local database, for each inventoried path, 
neither a hostile actor nor the user it targets gains or regains membership without authorization (including a grant 
racing a committed revocation), client DELETE behaves as the recorded choice, and the legitimate creation, join, 
rejoin, preference, archive, and read flows of the current, Phase 4, and hosted builds still work.
spec-interview/phase-5-messaging-resilience/PLAN.md-40-Verify by: Messaging DB integration tests (PostgREST as anon, 
as an authenticated outsider, and as a current member; real routes) asserting the complete membership delta and the 
affected users' access (403 on create/fence/roster, no messages/mentions): own INSERT into another conversation; 
UPDATE moving a row to another conversation/user; a member adding an outsider through `participants`; conversation 
INSERT naming arbitrary/cross-company participants or an unauthorized private room; `room_id`/`type` retargeting 
followed by resolver/join calls; removal → another user's legitimate join → removed user still denied, then an 
authorized rejoin restores access; preference and archive mutations held after authorization, membership deleted, then 
released → no replacement row and access still denied; creation, join, and rejoin held after their access check, the 
user's room access or membership revoked and committed, then released → no membership and access still denied (current 
routes and the legacy user-scoped write); self-DELETE yields the recorded membership delta (refused, or leave followed 
by a rejoin that requires authorization) and deleting another user's membership is denied; legitimate flows and 
old-build contracts (user-scoped conversation creation, preference/archive upserts of an existing membership) still 
pass; `npm run test:messaging:local:e2e` passes; presence-safety reviewer and the Presence DB suite run if any 
room/Presence/Knock path is touched; RLS review gate recorded.
